# Response headers for the webclient PWA on app.recued.com.
#
# ⛔ WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Audited live 2026-08-08: https://app.recued.com/
# returned 200 with NO HSTS, NO CSP, NO frame-ancestors / X-Frame-Options, NO
# Referrer-Policy and NO nosniff — a cross-origin iframe embedded the app
# successfully — while http://app.recued.com/ served a byte-identical shell over
# plaintext. Meanwhile the self-host LAN handler
# (`backend/server/src/webclient-handler.ts`) has sent a strict CSP since D-152
# § A.16, and its comment calls that "the same posture as `app.recued.com`'s
# public CSP". There was no public CSP. Same bytes, same threats — the two
# surfaces need the same headers, and this is the public half.
#
# ⚠ SYNTAX. Blocks are `pattern` at column 0 followed by INDENTED
# `Name: value` lines. Comments must also start at column 0 — an indented `#`
# line would be read as a header name. Max 100 rules, 2000 chars per line.
#
# ⚠ HOW IT APPLIES. This worker is ASSETS-ONLY (no `main` in wrangler.toml), so
# every response IS a static asset and this file covers all of them. Cloudflare
# does NOT apply `_headers` to responses generated by Worker code — irrelevant
# today, but the day this grows a `main`, headers stop applying to whatever that
# code returns. `build.mjs` copies `public/` into `build/`, the assets
# directory, so this ships without a build change.
#
# ⚠ WHAT THIS CANNOT DO. HSTS only protects a browser that has ALREADY reached
# the site over HTTPS once; it cannot secure a first visit or a cold cache. The
# plaintext listener itself is a ZONE setting — turn on "Always Use HTTPS" for
# recued.com in the Cloudflare dashboard. Neither recued.com nor app.recued.com
# is HSTS-preloaded, and `preload` is deliberately omitted below: submitting to
# the preload list is irreversible on browser timescales and should be a
# decision made once, on purpose, not a side effect of adding a header.

/*
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; connect-src 'self' ws: wss: http: https:; img-src 'self' data: blob:; font-src 'self'; manifest-src 'self'; worker-src 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin-allow-popups

# Notes on the block above, kept out of it so nothing is mistaken for a header:
#
# · Content-Security-Policy MIRRORS `WEBCLIENT_CSP` in
#   backend/server/src/webclient-handler.ts. Keep the two in step — they serve
#   the same bundle, and a directive present on only one of them means the other
#   is the surface that gets attacked. `script-src 'self'` carries NO
#   'unsafe-inline': index.html has no inline script, which is precisely why
#   boot-shell.js exists.
# · connect-src is scheme-bounded rather than wildcarded, and is wide by
#   necessity: the user types their own server URL and the webclient is a thin
#   client over it, so every RPC leaves for a host only the user knows.
# · X-Frame-Options duplicates frame-ancestors for older browsers. A live
#   cross-origin iframe embed is what the audit actually demonstrated.
# · Referrer-Policy matters here because the app is hash-routed — a full
#   referrer would leak which surface the user was on.
# · Permissions-Policy denies capabilities no part of this app uses, so a
#   compromised dependency cannot quietly start one.
# · ⛔ Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy is `same-origin-allow-popups`, NOT
#   `same-origin`. `same-origin` (2026-08-08 to 2026-09-28) cut every sign-in
#   popup off from the app the moment it opened Google's or Microsoft's page:
#   the callback page then had no `window.opener` to hand the code to, the
#   popup still said "Sign-in complete", and the app saw the popup as closed.
#   Gmail, Google Calendar and Outlook could not be connected from here at all.
#   `same-origin-allow-popups` still keeps other sites' windows away from this
#   app, and keeps the popups the app itself opens. The callback page drops the
#   header entirely (rule below): a popup whose final page sends a stricter
#   policy than its opener is moved away from the opener all the same.

# Pin the manifest's type rather than letting it be inferred from the extension —
# it is the file whose whole job is install identity.
/manifest.webmanifest
  Content-Type: application/manifest+json

# The OAuth callback page (served for /oauth-callback from oauth-callback.html)
# must keep its link to the app that opened it — see the COOP note above. No
# COOP here means `unsafe-none`, the one value that stays with a
# `same-origin-allow-popups` opener. `! ` removes the header the `/*` rule set;
# setting it again would join both values with a comma instead.
/oauth-callback
  ! Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy

/oauth-callback.html
  ! Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy
